<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · date unknown · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Under der linden</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song-poem from the Middle Ages by the German poet Walther von der Vogelweide, with a word in it that copies a nightingale's song.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Walther von der Vogelweide</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (the melody is lost)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>medieval German courtly lyric poetry</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>the lifetime of Walther von der Vogelweide</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by the medieval poet Walther von der Vogelweide)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/under-der-linden">Under der linden</a>&quot; is a well-known poem by the medieval German lyric poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a>, written in Middle High German. It was a song: its four strophes are preserved in only two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the Weingarten Manuscript and the Manesse Codex.</p><p>Neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> holds a melody, so the tune Walther's audience heard is not known. The melody of an anonymous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> song, &quot;En mai au douz tens novels&quot;, fits the metre of the poem, and this suggests that &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/under-der-linden">Under der linden</a>&quot; may be a contrafactum: new words written to an existing French tune. One word of the poem imitates the song of the nightingale.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of four strophes, in which one word imitates the song of the nightingale. The mood is light and musical.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Middle High German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Middle High German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Under der linden</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under the linden</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">an der heide,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on the heath,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dâ unser zweier bette was,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where the bed of us two was,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dâ muget ir vinden</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there you may find,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">schône beide</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lovely, both</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">gebrochen bluomen unde gras.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>broken flowers and grass.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vor dem walde in einem tal,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Before the forest, in a valley,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">tandaradei,*</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>tandaradei,*</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">schône sanc diu nahtegal.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sweetly sang the nightingale.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Ich kam gegangen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I came walking</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">zuo der ouwe:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the meadow:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dô was mîn friedel komen ê.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my beloved had come there before.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dâ wart ich empfangen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There I was received,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">hêre frouwe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>noble lady! —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">daz ich bin sælic iemer mê.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that I am blessed for evermore.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">kust er mich? wol tûsentstunt:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Did he kiss me? A thousand times, surely:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">tandaradei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>tandaradei,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">seht wie rôt mir ist der munt.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>see how red my mouth is.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Dô hete er gemachet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then he had made,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">alsô rîche</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so richly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">von bluomen eine bettestat.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a bed of flowers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">des wirt noch gelachet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>At that there will still be laughter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">inneclîche,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">kumt iemen an daz selbe pfat.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if anyone comes along that same path.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">bî den rôsen er wol mac</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the roses he may well,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">tandaradei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>tandaradei,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">merken wâ mirz houbet lac.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make out where my head lay.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Daz er bî mir læge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That he lay with me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wesse ez iemen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if anyone knew it</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">(nu enwelle got!), so schamte ich mich.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>(may God forbid it now!), I would be ashamed.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wes er mit mir pflæge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What he did with me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">niemer niemen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>never, no one</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">bevinde daz wan er und ich</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>shall find that out but he and I</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">und ein kleinez vogellîn:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and a little bird:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">tandaradei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>tandaradei,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">daz mac wol getriuwe sîn.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that one can surely be trusted.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Walther von der Vogelweide; the Middle High German text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_der_linden" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>'tandaradei' imitates the nightingale's song and is kept as it is; the asterisk is in the source.</li><li>2.5: 'hêre frouwe' is disputed: an exclamation ('noble Lady!', perhaps 'Holy Virgin!') or 'as a noble lady' (she was received like a fine lady).</li><li>2.6: 'sælic' is blessed, happy, blissful.</li><li>3.4-3.5: 'des wirt noch gelachet inneclîche' is literally 'at that there will still be laughing, inwardly / heartily'.</li><li>4.9: 'getriuwe' is faithful, loyal: the bird will keep the secret.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
